.TH VECTRA9 2
.SH NAME
Msg, Kind, Qid, Fid, Tag, encode, decode, message_size, Session, Transport, Handler, Errno \- the 9P2000.L message layer
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B import "vsys:vectra9"
.PP
.nf
.B "Fid :: distinct u32"
.B "Tag :: distinct u16"
.B "Qid :: struct { kind: Qid_Flags, version: u32, path: u64 }"
.B "Kind :: enum u8 { Rlerror = 7, ..., Tremove = 122, Rremove = 123 }"
.B "Msg :: union { Rlerror, Tversion, Rversion, Tattach, Twalk, Tread, Rread, Twrite, ... }"
.PP
.B "kind :: proc(msg: Msg) -> Kind"
.B "kind_name :: proc(k: Kind) -> string"
.B "is_request :: proc(kind: Kind) -> bool"
.B "creates :: proc(k: Kind) -> bool"
.PP
.B "encode :: proc(buf: []u8, tag: Tag, msg: Msg) -> (n: int, err: Error)"
.B "decode :: proc(data: []u8) -> (tag: Tag, msg: Msg, err: Error)"
.B "message_size :: proc(data: []u8) -> (size: u32, ok: bool)"
.PP
.B "Handler :: #type proc(server: rawptr, s: ^Session, tag: Tag, request: ^Msg, reply: ^Msg, buf: []u8)"
.B "Transport :: struct { data: rawptr, call: proc(...) -> Error, call_for: proc(...) -> Error }"
.B "Session :: struct { transport: Transport, msize: u32, version: string, next_tag: Tag, next_fid: Fid }"
.B "session_from :: proc(transport: Transport) -> Session"
.B "negotiate :: proc(s: ^Session, msize: u32 = 8192) -> Error"
.B "call :: proc(s: ^Session, request: ^Msg, reply: ^Msg, buf: []u8 = nil) -> Error"
.PP
.B "Errno :: distinct u32"
.B "error_reply :: proc(code: Errno) -> Msg"
.B "errno_name :: proc(code: Errno) -> string"
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
Vectra9 is the message layer of Vectra's file protocol.
The wire it serialises to, when it serialises at all, is
.BR 9P2000.L ,
unmodified: nothing here invents a message, adds a field to one, or
negotiates a private version string.
.PP
.B Msg
is a decoded message, an Odin tagged union over the fifty-odd 9P2000.L
bodies, one struct per message such as
.B Tattach
or
.BR Rread .
Every participant, in the kernel or out of it, produces and consumes
these; a
.BR devfs (3)
read of
.B /dev/cons
builds a
.BR Tread ,
hands it to a handler, and gets an
.B Rread
back, with no buffer, no parse and no copy of the payload.
.I Kind
is the wire's own numbering of a message, in
.IR kind ;
.I is_request
reports whether a kind is a
.B T
message, and
.I creates
reports whether one would make, rename or link a file.
.PP
.B "A decoded message borrows its buffer."
Strings and byte slices inside a
.B Msg
point into whatever it was decoded from, or, for a message built by
hand, into whatever the builder owns.
They are valid exactly as long as that memory is, and no part of this
package copies to make that easier.
This is what lets an
.B Rread
of a full buffer cost nothing to pass around, and it is the rule most
easily broken: a caller that reuses the bytes a
.B Msg
still points into has corrupted a live reply.
The rule holds for one request at a time; a transport that runs several
at once gives each one storage of its own and passes it as
.IR buf ,
so the reply borrows the request rather than a buffer another handler is
already writing into.
.PP
.I Encode
turns a tagged
.B Msg
into wire bytes in
.IR buf ,
framed as
.B size[4] type[1] tag[2]
then the body, with
.I size
counting itself.
.I Decode
is the reverse.
.I Message_size
reads just the four-byte size prefix of a header, to tell a caller how
much more to read before calling
.IR decode .
Only a transport that crosses an address space calls any of the three;
an in-process call passes the
.B Msg
by pointer and never serialises.
.PP
A
.B Session
is one client's conversation with one server: a negotiated
.IR msize ,
a fid space, a tag space and a
.BR Transport .
.I Session_from
builds one over a transport, and
.I negotiate
performs the
.B Tversion
handshake, taking the smaller of the offered and the answered
.IR msize .
.I Call
sends one request and waits for its reply, allocating a tag from the
session.
A
.B Handler
is what a server presents: given a request it always produces a reply,
even when the answer is no, which is what
.B Rlerror
is for.
.PP
.B Errno
is the protocol's own error vocabulary, carried in an
.B Rlerror
reply as a Linux errno number rather than the text plain 9P used.
.I Error_reply
builds the
.B Rlerror
for a given code, and
.I errno_name
renders one for a log.
It is a separate vocabulary from the codec's own
.B Error
type: an
.B Errno
means the request was well-formed and the answer is no, where a codec
.B Error
means the bytes themselves cannot be turned into a reply at all.
.SS The message table
All of 9P2000.L, in the encoding every implementation uses:
.B size[4] type[1] tag[2]
then the body, with
.I size
counting itself.
A string,
.BR s ,
is a two-byte length then that many bytes, never NUL-terminated. A qid
is thirteen bytes.
.IP
.EX
Rlerror        ecode[4]
Tstatfs/Rstatfs        fid[4] / type[4] bsize[4] blocks[8] bfree[8] ...
Tlopen/Rlopen  fid[4] flags[4] / qid[13] iounit[4]
Tlcreate/Rlcreate      fid[4] name[s] flags[4] mode[4] gid[4] / qid[13] iounit[4]
Tsymlink/Rsymlink      fid[4] name[s] symtgt[s] gid[4] / qid[13]
Tmknod/Rmknod  dfid[4] name[s] mode[4] major[4] minor[4] gid[4] / qid[13]
Trename/Rrename        fid[4] dfid[4] name[s] / \(em
Treadlink/Rreadlink    fid[4] / target[s]
Tgetattr/Rgetattr      fid[4] request_mask[8] / valid[8] qid[13] ...
Tsetattr/Rsetattr      fid[4] valid[4] mode[4] uid[4] gid[4] size[8] ... / \(em
Txattrwalk/Rxattrwalk  fid[4] newfid[4] name[s] / size[8]
Txattrcreate/Rxattrcreate      fid[4] name[s] attr_size[8] flags[4] / \(em
Treaddir/Rreaddir      fid[4] offset[8] count[4] / count[4] data[count]
Tfsync/Rfsync  fid[4] datasync[4] / \(em
Tlock/Rlock    fid[4] type[1] flags[4] start[8] length[8] proc_id[4] client_id[s] / status[1]
Tgetlock/Rgetlock      fid[4] type[1] start[8] length[8] proc_id[4] client_id[s] / same
Tlink/Rlink    dfid[4] fid[4] name[s] / \(em
Tmkdir/Rmkdir  dfid[4] name[s] mode[4] gid[4] / qid[13]
Trenameat/Rrenameat    olddirfid[4] oldname[s] newdirfid[4] newname[s] / \(em
Tunlinkat/Runlinkat    dirfid[4] name[s] flags[4] / \(em
Tversion/Rversion      msize[4] version[s] / same
Tauth/Rauth    afid[4] uname[s] aname[s] n_uname[4] / aqid[13]
Tattach/Rattach        fid[4] afid[4] uname[s] aname[s] n_uname[4] / qid[13]
Tflush/Rflush  oldtag[2] / \(em
Twalk/Rwalk    fid[4] newfid[4] nwname[2] names[s]... / nwqid[2] qids[13]...
Tread/Rread    fid[4] offset[8] count[4] / count[4] data[count]
Twrite/Rwrite  fid[4] offset[8] count[4] data[count] / count[4]
Tclunk/Rclunk  fid[4] / \(em
Tremove/Rremove        fid[4] / \(em
.EE
.PP
Absent, because 9P2000.L replaced them:
.BR Topen ,
.BR Tcreate ,
.BR Tstat ,
.BR Twstat ,
.BR Rerror .
Semantics for each message are in section 5:
.IR version (5),
.IR attach (5),
.IR flush (5),
.IR walk (5),
.IR open (5),
.IR read (5),
.IR getattr (5)
and
.IR clunk (5).
Errors are
.IR error (5).
.SH EXAMPLES
A client negotiates over an in-process transport and then calls:
.IP
.EX
session := vectra9.session_from(vectra9.in_process_transport(&direct))
vectra9.negotiate(&session)
err := vectra9.call(&session, &request, &reply, buf[:])
.EE
.PP
A server answers a
.B Tversion
the way every server in this tree does:
.IP
.EX
case vectra9.Tversion:
	vectra9.version_reply(m, reply, FRAME)
.EE
